($t$Inocarpus fagifer$t$, $t$Inocarpus fagifer$t$, $t$Fabaceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Fabaceae. Portamento: Tree. Altezza massima indicativa: 20 m. Inocarpus fagifer or commonly known as Tahiti Chestnut is an evergreen tree exhibiting a large and dense canopy, and short, thick, irregular and buttressed bole of up to 90 cm in diameter. It grows usually about 20 m in height. It has a shallow taproot and well-formed network of lateral roots. It is grown in East Asia for its edible seeds. The leaves are dark green, oblong, and leathery. The white to cream or pale yellow flowers are fragrant and form into clusters at the ends of branches and twigs. The plant is fairly shade tolerant, moderately to fast-growing, and lives up to 90 years. Flowering starts at 3 - 5 years old. The seeds can be eaten raw or cooked (boiled or baked). Tahiti chestnut is also used in traditional medicine particularly in the treatment of sickness relapses, burns, diarrhea, scabies, teething problems in infants, pneumonia, bone fractures, stomach pains, bone pains, malarial fever, and internal bleeding. The wood is fairly durable but may be susceptible to termites as sawn timber. It is used for light construction, canoes, crafts, and tool handles among others.$t$, $j${"luce": "Tollera: pieno sole, mezz'ombra, ombra", "acqua": "Abbondante, terreno costantemente umido", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: sabbioso/leggero, medio, argilloso/pesante"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Tahiti Chestnut, Polynesian Chestnut$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): None known$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "npk": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}}$j$::jsonb, $j${"famiglia_botanica": "Fabaceae", "giorni_germinazione": null, "giorni_trapianto": null, "giorni_raccolta": null, "finestra_semina": [], "finestra_trapianto": [], "resistenza_gelo": "bassa", "spaziatura_cm": null, "consociazioni_favorevoli": [], "consociazioni_sfavorevoli": []}$j$::jsonb, $j${"adatta": null, "dimensione_minima_litri": null, "rinvaso_ogni_mesi": null, "irrigazione_fattore": null}$j$::jsonb, $t$perenne$t$, $t$inocarpus-fagifer$t$, ARRAY[$t$Plants For A Future (pfaf.org) — importazione bozza automatica, dati non verificati manualmente$t$], $t$bozza$t$, null, null),
($t$Intsia bijuga$t$, $t$Intsia bijuga$t$, $t$Fabaceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Fabaceae. Portamento: Tree. Altezza massima indicativa: 25 m. Other common names include Kwila, Johnston River Teak, Ipil, Merbau, and Scrub Mahogany. Intsia bijuga or commonly known as Moluccan Ironwood is a medium-sized flowering tree that can be found on sand and coral beaches and dry mangroves in East Africa and East Asia. It grows up to 50 m tall with a buttressed trunk and spreading crown. The leaves are shiny green and the flowers are fragrant. The bark is used in the treatment of urinary conditions, rheumatism, dysentery and diarrhoea. The seeds are edible when boiled after being soaked in water for 3-4 days. The tree has an extensive root system making it an ideal species for soil conservation and reclamation. It is also used as a windbreak as it is highly tolerant to strong winds. The wood and bark yields brown dye. The bark is a source of tannins. The seed oil has the capability to repel insects and is used to protect stored products. The wood is heavy, hard to very hard, strong, durable, and resistant to attacks of insects, fungi, and wood borers. It is used for bridge-making, railway sleepers, boat building, house post beams, and furniture.$t$, $j${"luce": "Tollera: pieno sole, mezz'ombra", "acqua": "Moderata", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: sabbioso/leggero, medio"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Moluccan Ironwood, Ipil$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): None known$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "npk": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}}$j$::jsonb, $j${"famiglia_botanica": "Fabaceae", "giorni_germinazione": null, "giorni_trapianto": null, "giorni_raccolta": null, "finestra_semina": [], "finestra_trapianto": [], "resistenza_gelo": "nessuna", "spaziatura_cm": null, "consociazioni_favorevoli": [], "consociazioni_sfavorevoli": []}$j$::jsonb, $j${"adatta": null, "dimensione_minima_litri": null, "rinvaso_ogni_mesi": null, "irrigazione_fattore": null}$j$::jsonb, $t$perenne$t$, $t$intsia-bijuga$t$, ARRAY[$t$Plants For A Future (pfaf.org) — importazione bozza automatica, dati non verificati manualmente$t$], $t$bozza$t$, null, null),
($t$Intsia palembanica$t$, $t$Intsia palembanica$t$, $t$Fabaceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Fabaceae. Portamento: Tree. Altezza massima indicativa: 80 m. Intsia palembanica or commonly known as Borneo Teak is a tree growing up to 50 m tall with trunk diameter of up to 120 cm. It can be found in forests in East Asia. Young seeds are edible. The bark and wood yields brown and yellow dyes used for coloring mats and clothes. The wood is heavy, hard, strong, durable and resistant to fungi, wood borers, and termites. It is used for high-grade construction work, interior finishing, panelling, parquet flooring, furniture, etc.$t$, $j${"luce": "Pieno sole", "acqua": "Moderata", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: sabbioso/leggero, medio"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Borneo Teak, Merbau$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): None known$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "npk": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}}$j$::jsonb, $j${"famiglia_botanica": "Fabaceae", "giorni_germinazione": null, "giorni_trapianto": null, "giorni_raccolta": null, "finestra_semina": [], "finestra_trapianto": [], "resistenza_gelo": "bassa", "spaziatura_cm": null, "consociazioni_favorevoli": [], "consociazioni_sfavorevoli": []}$j$::jsonb, $j${"adatta": null, "dimensione_minima_litri": null, "rinvaso_ogni_mesi": null, "irrigazione_fattore": null}$j$::jsonb, $t$perenne$t$, $t$intsia-palembanica$t$, ARRAY[$t$Plants For A Future (pfaf.org) — importazione bozza automatica, dati non verificati ma